WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.190
So a capital allocator, explain
what that job is in the world,

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:08.280
and do you have any, I was thinking about
this today when I was in the shower,

00:00:08.281 --> 00:00:12.810
I was like, where did this concept of
capital allocators even originate from, I,

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:16.320
we know about carry and
that when, you know,

00:00:16.321 --> 00:00:21.030
ships used to go to the what
was then called the orient or to

00:00:21.031 --> 00:00:24.510
India to get spice or whatever
somebody would say, hey, listen,

00:00:24.511 --> 00:00:26.040
if you get the package back here,

00:00:26.041 --> 00:00:28.920
I'll give you 20% of whatever makes
it back to shore. You get carry,

00:00:28.921 --> 00:00:31.320
you get a portion of what comes
back and that aligned interest.

00:00:31.321 --> 00:00:32.190
So we know that piece,

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:35.250
where did the concept of a
capital allocator even come from?

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:39.440
I think it's relatively new. If I go back,

00:00:39.441 --> 00:00:43.670
I started my career in 1992. And just
to give you a perspective at the time,

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:47.600
there were no more than a half a
dozen of these offices in the country.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:53.180
And I think I was the only junior
analysts looking at funds in

00:00:53.181 --> 00:00:56.270
the entire country, like Yale
used to hire one undergrad a year.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.000
And so Harvard had an office,

00:00:59.001 --> 00:01:01.700
but they were mostly direct
investing and Stanford had an office,

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:04.730
but there were four or five, you
know, senior people. There are very,

00:01:04.731 --> 00:01:05.564
very few of them.

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:11.120
The reason I think it's come to pass
has kind of goes hand in hand with the

00:01:11.121 --> 00:01:15.920
notion of modern portfolio
theory in that if you

00:01:15.921 --> 00:01:19.340
have a large pool of capital and you're
trying to preserve and grow it for a

00:01:19.341 --> 00:01:23.960
long period of time and their
spending needs along the way,

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:27.170
people have adopted the notion
that you want to be diversified.

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:31.490
And so the question is, if you
were 4 or 5 people, you know,

00:01:31.491 --> 00:01:33.830
maybe it was 10 or 12 back
then and a little more today,

00:01:33.831 --> 00:01:36.350
but you're 4 or 5 people sitting
in new Haven, Connecticut,

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.890
and you want to diversify around the
world. You have two options, right?

00:01:39.891 --> 00:01:41.120
One is you could say, okay,

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:45.380
let's go figure out how to compete
with Jason and all the other seed stage

00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:47.630
venture capitalist, and
we'll compete with Sequoia.

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:51.320
And then let's go to Asia and what
we'll pick Chinese stocks. And Oh,

00:01:51.321 --> 00:01:51.711
by the way,

00:01:51.711 --> 00:01:55.040
we probably want to be in large cap and
small cap stocks in the US and equity

00:01:55.041 --> 00:01:58.430
and credit, Oh, we need expertise in
the bond markets. At some point in time,

00:01:58.431 --> 00:02:00.620
your head starts spinning and say,
well, we can do one of two things.

00:02:00.621 --> 00:02:04.910
We can either try to compete with
an increasingly specialized world,

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:07.070
or we can just go try to identify them.

00:02:07.071 --> 00:02:10.670
Let's develop our skillset to try to
identify the best and partner with them.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:12.050
And so that,

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:17.420
that became a model that went hand in
hand with this kind of strategy and really

00:02:17.421 --> 00:02:18.590
got popularized

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:21.950
when David Swenson wrote his kind
of seminal book called portfolio,

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:24.170
Pioneering Portfolio Management in 2000,

00:02:24.171 --> 00:02:28.730
and then you started to see proliferation
of more of these offices and more LPs

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:31.670
and these strategies. And so the,

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:36.500
the clear path to becoming a capital
allocator at one of these endowments

00:02:36.501 --> 00:02:41.090
was to have been an alumni of that
place for some reason. Is that correct?

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:43.190
I mean, I suppose right there,

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:46.250
one of the things that's interesting
about my show is when I interview a bunch

00:02:46.251 --> 00:02:48.530
of these CIOs, there is no clear path.

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:52.640
Because the fact is the industry
didn't exist 20 years ago.

00:02:52.641 --> 00:02:56.720
So how could you have been trained in
it? Ah, yes. That makes total sense.

00:02:56.721 --> 00:03:01.540
It's like asking for somebody to be
like a, an AR developer it's like,

00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:03.940
or, you know, an AI developer. Well, yeah,

00:03:03.941 --> 00:03:06.220
it's been accessible now for 30
years, but yes, people were like,

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:11.260
I need a iPhone app developer with five
years experience when the app store

00:03:11.261 --> 00:03:12.094
had just came out,

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:15.480
people wouldn't put up ads for that
because it doesn't make any sense.

